<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
    The toggle inside a preference row.

    Deliberately the same SwitchCompat, with the same explicit attributes, as the accessory in
    layout_settings_row.xml - the switch on Accord's own Settings page. Two reasons:

    1. showText. One UI's platform switch style turns it on, and a bare switch inherits that: the
       track and thumb went unpainted and the row showed a tiny "OFF" instead of a toggle. Blanking
       textOn/textOff as well means an OEM that ignores showText still has nothing to draw.
    2. The tints. These screens are inflated against Theme.Gramophone (Material 3) while the shell
       around them is Material 2, so a theme-resolved toggle here would not match the one two taps
       away on the Settings root. Naming the colours makes the host theme irrelevant.
-->
<androidx.appcompat.widget.SwitchCompat xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:id="@+id/switchWidget"
    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:background="@null"
    android:clickable="false"
    android:focusable="false"
    android:textOff=""
    android:textOn=""
    app:showText="false"
    app:thumbTint="@color/cl_settings_switch_thumb"
    app:trackTint="@color/cl_settings_switch_track"
    app:trackTintMode="src_in" />
